# MeterWorklog Architecture Overview

## System Architecture

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                          BROWSER (Frontend)                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  index.html (SPA) + static/app.js loader + style.css    │    │
│  │  Tabs: Dashboard / Worklogs / File Share / Settings     │    │
│  │  Frontend modules under static/app/:                    │    │
│  │  • i18n.js, core.js, dashboard.js, worklogs.js          │    │
│  │  • calendar.js, export.js, settings.js, files.js        │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
└──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┘
                       │ JSON REST API (HTTPS, multipart for uploads)
                       │
┌──────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  FLASK SERVER (Backend, app/)                   │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │              Flask App (app/__init__.py)                  │  │
│  │  • Session management, login lockout, DB initialization  │  │
│  │  • CSRF-origin check, upload size cap, storage config    │  │
│  │  • Blueprint registration                                │  │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                           │                                      │
│  ┌───────────┬────────────┼────────────┬───────────┬─────────┐  │
│  ▼    ▼      ▼      ▼     ▼     ▼      ▼     ▼     ▼         │  │
│ auth members projects worklogs users exports core files       │  │
│                                                                  │
│  • Each module = Blueprint with routes                          │
│  • Decorators: @login_required, @elevated_required              │
│  • Returns JSON (or streamed bytes for Excel / file download)   │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │              helpers.py, constants.py                    │  │
│  │  • parse_time, format_members, parse_members             │  │
│  │  • Role constants (ROLE_STAFF, ROLE_ADMIN, etc.)         │  │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
└─────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┘
          │ SQL via pyodbc                             │ os/shutil
          │                                            │
┌─────────┴─────────────────────────┐   ┌──────────────┴──────────┐
│       MS SQL SERVER Database      │   │   Local Disk Storage    │
│                                    │   │                          │
│  Tables:                           │   │  storage/files/          │
│  • members                         │   │    YYYY/MM/<uuid>.bin    │
│  • users                           │   │                          │
│  • projects                        │   │  • Blob filenames are    │
│  • worklogs (hours is computed)    │   │    UUIDs (never user     │
│  • settings (feature flags)        │   │    input — path-traversal│
│  • file_folders (self-ref tree)    │   │    proof)                │
│  • files (metadata; blobs on disk) │   │  • Original filename &   │
│                                    │   │    MIME stored in DB     │
│  db.py: query(), execute()         │   │  • send_file streams out │
│  init_db.sql: schema + migrations  │   │    with Content-         │
│                                    │   │    Disposition=attachment│
└────────────────────────────────────┘   └──────────────────────────┘
```

---

## Data Flow Examples

### User Login
```
Browser (login.html)
  ↓ POST /api/login {username, password}
Flask (auth.py → login_handler)
  ↓ query users table, verify password
SQL Server
  ↓ return user record
Flask → set session['user_id'], session['role'], etc.
  ↓ return {role, member_id}
Browser → store in currentUser, redirect to app
```

### Add Worklog
```
Browser (app.js → api POST /api/worklogs)
  ↓ {member_id, log_date, project, task, start_time, end_time, status}
Flask (worklogs.py)
  ↓ validate permissions (staff can only edit own; elevated can edit any)
  ↓ INSERT into worklogs table
SQL Server
  ↓ compute hours = DATEDIFF(MINUTE, start, end) / 60.0
Flask → return {ok: true}
  ↓ Browser → refresh worklog table
```

### Export to Excel
```
Browser (app.js → openMonthFilterModal)
  ↓ User selects months + clicks Download
  ↓ GET /api/export/excel?member_id=X&year=Y&months=1,3,6
Flask (exports.py → generate_excel_bytes)
  ↓ Query worklogs for member + year
  ↓ Load template XLSX (empty sheet)
  ↓ For each selected month: copy empty → month name sheet
  ↓ Populate month sheets with worklog rows
  ↓ Delete empty sheet, return binary
Browser → file download
```

### Upload File
```
Browser (files.js → uploadFiles)
  ↓ Drag-drop or click Upload → multipart POST /api/files/upload
  ↓ Body: file + form field folder_id (optional; null = root)
Flask (files.py → upload_file)
  ↓ Check storage guards:
  │   • free-disk floor (shutil.disk_usage)
  │   • global cap (SUM(files.size_bytes))
  │   → HTTP 507 if either tripped
  ↓ Generate UUID stored_name → write to storage/files/YYYY/MM/<uuid>.bin
  │   • Stream in 64 KB chunks, compute SHA-256 inline
  │   • Write to <uuid>.bin.part, then os.replace() atomic rename
  ↓ INSERT into files table (folder_id, original_name, stored_name,
      size_bytes, mime_type, sha256, uploaded_by)
Flask → {id, original_name, size_bytes, ...}
  ↓ Browser → refresh folder contents + usage card
```

### Download File
```
Browser → <a href="/api/files/123/download">Download</a>
Flask (files.py → download_file)
  ↓ Query files table by id → original_name, stored_name, mime_type
  ↓ _resolve_blob_path() — re-check resolved path is inside FILE_STORAGE_DIR
  │   (defense against stored_name ever being tampered with)
  ↓ send_file(abs_path, as_attachment=True,
              download_name=original_name, mimetype=mime_type)
Browser → browser saves file with original name (RFC 5987 encoded header)
```

